  "npm test -- --test-name-pattern \"multiple heredoc payloads all remain data\"",project,pi-agent,"2026-08-31T12:32:47.043Z",local_mutation,fix/index-unquoted-scalar-assignments
  "npm test -- --test-name-pattern \"heredoc payload syntax does not change scalar scope\"",project,pi-agent,"2026-08-31T12:32:47.043Z",local_mutation,fix/index-unquoted-scalar-assignments
close_reason: "All three heredoc tests restructured onto realistic run: | fixtures whose publishes route through post-terminator scalars, so each assertion now fails unless the terminator is recognised and fails again if the payload is indexed - the vacuity is gone in both directions."
body: "CodeRabbit, Major, test/verify-release-publish-attestation.test.ts:1072-1081 (PR 84):\n\n\"**Add a positive assertion that the heredoc actually terminates.** — Each heredoc test expects one failure. That result holds in both directions: if the heredoc closes, `FLAG` is scoped out and `npm publish \\$FLAG` fails; if the heredoc never closes, `FLAG` is also unindexed and the same publish fails. So these cases do not prove that the indented terminator ends the payload.\n\nAssert the closing behavior directly by indexing a scalar after the terminator. This is the coverage that would expose the termination gap reported at scripts/shell-command-scan.ts lines 634-637.\"\n\nThe same vacuity was confirmed in a third heredoc test (\"heredoc payload syntax does not change scalar scope\"), which shares the criterion: its heredoc never closes under any correct implementation of shell semantics, so scope stays trivially unchanged. All three received the same treatment."
